Answers and their paragraphs (each of the reader model's answers 1 to 6 beside the story-view paragraph the author marked as carrying it):
answer 1: What happened: On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court granted review of whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) requires detention, during removal proceedings, of people who, like Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha, are in the United States and were not admitted. (`scotus-order-list-2026-10-01`; `scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:49: On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. Admitted means the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. The detention statute deems a person present in the United States who has not been admitted an applicant for admission.
answer 2: Why it matters to someone who does not follow this subject: A person who has lived here for more than twenty years, with an asylum application and a work permit, was arrested on his way to work and later released on bond only after a judge found he was not a danger or a flight risk; if the government's reading wins, people in that situation can be held for the whole removal case without that hearing, and by the grant the dispute was no longer one man's case or the two appeals courts named in April. (`ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28`; `audit-gap-ca4-2026-09-10`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:50: The bond statute says the government may keep an arrested person in custody or may release that person on bond. A hearing is a chance at release, not release itself. An immigration judge found that Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha presents no danger to persons or property and no risk of flight, and released him on bond. He entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond under the detention statute. The Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond".
answer 3: The easiest wrong reading, the one the headline or the coverage invites: That the Court is deciding whether the administration may detain tens of thousands of people with no hearing at all, as a yes-or-no on a detention policy, and that the grant means the detention is now the law. (`cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01`; `scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:87: CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. No court or agency record gives the number of people detained because of the July 2025 change. The Fourth Circuit says district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention.
answer 4: The one concept a stranger must hold to follow it, the mechanism: The detention statute says an applicant for admission shall be detained when an alien seeking admission is not clearly entitled to be admitted; the Second Circuit held that a man who entered years ago may be an applicant for admission and still not be seeking admission, so the bond statute applies, and that statute says the government may release him. (`ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28`; `uscode-2024-1225`; `uscode-2024-1226`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:58: The Second Circuit held that he may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization. He was already living here. The court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone already in the United States who entered without inspection and was not caught while entering or shortly afterward. It affirmed the district court's order, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole, and the parole statute allows that release "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit".
answer 5: What changed: A 1997 rule stated bond eligibility for people present without admission, and the Second Circuit describes that practice as thirty years old; in July 2025 the government said section 235, not the bond section, applies to all applicants for admission; the Board said judges lack authority to grant bond; the Second Circuit held the opposite on April 28 and on September 25 refused to rehear the case, with a dissent and a concurrence that agreed with the dissent's analysis; on October 1 the Supreme Court granted review. (`audit-gap-fr-1997-03-06`; `ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28`; `cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10`; `bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05`; `ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25`; `audit-gap-ca2-enbanc-complete`; `scotus-order-list-2026-10-01`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:103: On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis. He wrote that mere substantive disagreement with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing.
answer 6: What is unresolved as of the newest dated record: As of October 5 the Court has not decided this case and the October 1 argument list sets no day; the question granted is the statute, not due process. Genalo v. G.M., a related constitutional case set for argument on October 13, was dismissed September 11. On September 21 the government filed Genalo v. D.C., and counsel wrote that the new petition presents the same constitutional issues, with a response due October 21. Counsel asked the Court to grant only the statutory question in this case and in Buenrostro-Mendez. On the October 5 dockets the Court had not granted or denied Lopez-Campos or Genalo v. D.C. The October 3 docket in Buenrostro-Mendez lists no grant or denial. The August 21 response text was posted October 5 and the September 4 reply is still listed without its text. (`scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05`; `scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01`; `scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01`; `doj-petition-26-104`; `scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-14`; `scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-25`; `scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05`; `scotus-docket-genalo-dc-2026-10-05`; `scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-05`; `scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03`)
  /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/src/pages/events/immigration-detention/2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.astro:127: No day for argument has been set. The Court agreed to decide what the detention statute requires, not whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process. The government's petition says Lopez-Campos, from the Sixth Circuit, also presents that due-process question. Genalo v. G.M., a related constitutional case set for argument on October 13, was dismissed on September 11. On September 21 the government filed a new petition, Genalo v. D.C., with a response due October 21, and Barbosa da Cunha's lawyer wrote that it presents the same constitutional issues as the dismissed case. On September 14 and September 25 that lawyer asked the Court to grant only the statutory question in this case and in Buenrostro-Mendez. The Court had not granted or denied Lopez-Campos or Genalo v. D.C. on the October 5 dockets. The October 3 docket in Buenrostro-Mendez, from the Fifth Circuit, listed no grant or denial.

