data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt-21-
data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt-22---- page 7 ---
data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt-23-4 Blackstone, Commentaries on the Laws of England 137 (1768) (emphasis omitted). In sum, the petition, in challenging the lawfulness of Petitioner’s detention and requesting release, sought habeas relief, which the district court provided by ordering release absent a prompt bond hearing. The dissent’s novel arguments to the contrary are unpersuasive. * * * As to the sole question presented on appeal—whether Section 1225 or Section 1226 governed Petitioner’s detention—our holding was dictated by the plain text of the statute. Section 1225(b)(2)(A) “applies only to a noncitizen who is both an ‘applicant for admission’ and ‘seeking admission.’” Barbosa da Cunha, 175 F.4th at 74. Petitioner, who entered the United States unlawfully more than two decades ago and has resided here ever since, was not “seeking admission” because he was not “seeking to come inside [the country] from outside.” Id. at 97 (Cabranes, J., concurring). Accordingly, his detention was governed by Section 1226, which entitled him to a bond hearing. The dissent’s “attempt to muddy these textually clear waters deﬁes the statute’s context, structure, history, and purpose[.]” Id. at 96 (majority opinion). The dissent’s novel argument about the scope of habeas fares no better. According to the dissent, the nine circuits and hundreds of district courts to grant habeas relief to individuals wrongly detained under Section 1225 did not have authority to do so. That is a drastic view that not even the government has advanced in this case. Because the district court was right on the statutory question and had authority to grant habeas relief, the panel opinion correctly aﬃrmed the decision below and our Court correctly voted to deny rehearing en banc.
