data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt-579-756 (Murphy, J., dissenting) (noting that the parole provision Congress adopted is broader than “the narrow version in the House Report” on which Petitioners and the majority rely). More fundamentally, this argument must be rejected because courts are not at liberty to “replace the actual text [of a statute] with speculation as to Congress’ intent.” Oklahoma v. Castro-Huerto, 142 S. Ct. 2486, 2496 (2022) (internal quotation marks omitted). “Congress expresses itself as a body through the text it enacts.” FS Credit Opportunities Corp. v. Saba Cap. Master Fund, Ltd, 146 S. Ct. 1546, 1558 (2026). The text of the law therefore “controls over purported legislative intentions unmoored from any statutory text.” Castro-Huerto, 142 S. Ct. at 2496; see also Henson v. Santander Consumer USA Inc., 582 U.S. 79, 89 (2017) (rejecting purposive speculation in favor of “apply[ing] faithfully the law Congress has written”). 4. Agency practice Finally, Petitioners and the majority emphasize that, for three decades, the Executive has detained aliens present in the United States without admission under Section 1226(a)’s discretionary scheme rather than Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory scheme. This practice tells us nothing about the meaning of the latter statute. Cf. Pereira v. Sessions, 585 U.S. 198, 204–205 (2018) (rejecting an interpretation of the INA that reflected 21 years of government practice); Pub. Emps. Ret. Sys. of Ohio v. Betts, 492 U.S. 158, 171 (1989) (“Even contemporaneous and longstanding agency interpretations must fall to the extent they conflict with statutory language.”). “Courts must exercise their independent judgment” in interpreting the law. Loper Bright Enters. v. Raimondo, 144 S. Ct. 2244, 2273 (2024). Although an agency’s reasoned
