4. **What is the dated path from changed practice to this grant?** The [1997 rule](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt) says those present without admission “will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination”; the [Customs memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) says “Effective immediately, it is the position of DHS”; the [Board decision](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05.txt) says “Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond”; the [appeals opinion](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt) is dated “Decided: April 28, 2026”; and the [order](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt) is dated “THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026.” This changes how **answer 5** is understood. One short passage near the top should carry that order, displacing the need to assemble it from “What happened,” the table, and “Where this sits.”

5. **How many people are actually detained solely under this reading?** **Unknown:** no admitted record answers it. The page distinguishes the Fourth Circuit’s case count from people held, but does not explicitly state that a count for this detention category is unknown. This bears on **answer 3** and displaces the repeated CBS display in line edit **5**.

