## 8\. Reconstructed Story

For decades after the 1996 overhaul, people arrested in the interior after entering without inspection were treated as eligible to seek bond. DHS changed that position in July 2025, the Board adopted the mandatory-detention interpretation, and Barbosa da Cunha was denied a hearing after his September arrest. A district court ordered a hearing or release, he obtained bond, and the Second Circuit held that an interior resident can be an “applicant for admission” without currently “seeking admission.” Other circuits split, and the Supreme Court granted Rhoney on October 1 to resolve that statutory question.

The constitutional question remains separate. The Court had granted a related prolonged-detention case, Genalo, but dismissed it September 11\. The government filed 26-379 as a replacement September 21\. Lopez-Campos also presents due-process issues connected to the current detention litigation. Rhoney itself does not put that constitutional question before the Court.

