## First read

The page told me that the Supreme Court took a dispute over whether people living in the country after entering without admission can be denied a bond hearing while their removal cases proceed.

I still wanted to know exactly who would lose a hearing if the government wins and whether the related petitions had moved. I first lost the thread at: “The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission.” The page had reached its legal distinction before I could readily explain it.

## The story

The record supports this account: a 1997 rule made people present without admission eligible to seek bond; DHS adopted a contrary detention reading in July 2025; Barbosa da Cunha was denied a hearing, then released on bond after a judge’s finding; appeals courts split; and on October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court granted review of which statute governs his detention. The [1997 rule](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt:203) says this group “will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.” The [July memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) assigns “section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236” to all applicants for admission. The [October 1 order](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt) says “The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.”

The page has the right angle and opening act. Its early dated passage gives the July memorandum, Board decision, arrest, denied hearing, district court order and April appeals ruling in order. The bond release appears in the preceding stakes paragraph, and the grant opens the section. That is enough to follow the sequence, though the reader must connect those adjacent paragraphs. The page states that the Court has not decided the merits and that this grant does not take the due process question.

Since the October 1 event, the respondent’s brief became available on October 5. The pinned [docket](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05.txt) records “Brief of respondent Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed.  (PDF upload - October 5, 2026)”; the page’s records account includes that upload. In the live [Rhoney](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html), [Lopez-Campos](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html), [Buenrostro-Mendez](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html) and [Genalo v. D.C.](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-379.html) proceedings checked for this memo, I found no later disposition that puts the page behind the event.

| Reader-model answer | Does the marked paragraph carry it? |
| --- | --- |
| 1. What happened | **Yes.** The first paragraph states the grant, its question and its limit. |
| 2. Why it matters | **Yes.** The stakes paragraph joins his long residence, the judge’s finding, bond release and the consequence of a government win. |
| 3. Easiest wrong reading | **Yes across the page.** The top distinguishes a grant from a ruling; the catch distinguishes court cases from people detained. |
| 4. Mechanism | **Yes, with effort.** The marked paragraph says he can be an applicant without seeking admission because he was already living here. The bond statute’s choice appears earlier. The parole sentence would work better beside the government’s reading. |
| 5. What changed | **No, as marked.** That paragraph carries the September rehearing dispute. The early dated passage and table carry the older practice, July change, Board decision, appeals ruling and grant. The answer is on the page; the mark sits on its final beat. |
| 6. What is unresolved | **Yes.** Its opening states the unset argument day and the limit of this statutory grant. The related-case detail then asks more work of the reader than the core answer does. |

